        "andreas_1885_v2_buildings"
      ],
      "note": "Read off the stereograph: a ground storey of round-arched shop fronts with striped awnings, six storeys in all (Andreas: 'six stories high'), the Randolph front in three groups of four bays and the Clark front in three groups of five between plain pilaster strips, the end groups' windows round-headed and the middle groups' square-headed under small cornices, a deep bracketed cornice, a continuous iron balcony at the first floor, a balustraded attic over the south-east corner with its flagstaff. Storey heights are read through the stereograph's camera (no dimension of them was found): fitted on the cornice's three corners and the front's two feet, the wall head stands about 30 m over the street (a 6.0 m shop storey, then 5.2 to 4.4 m). The open iron railings of the first-floor gallery, the third-floor balcony over the middle four bays of the Randolph front, the Clark Street portico's two-storey columns under its railed balcony and the attic's stone balustrade are read off the same stereograph (T-3688)."
